<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pyrgi</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pyrgi was the chief port of the Etruscan city of Caere, on the Tyrrhenian coast at modern Santa Severa, north-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. Its sanctuary held temples of the goddess Uni, where in 1964 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrgi-tablets">Pyrgi Tablets</a> were found: gold sheets of about 500 BCE recording in Etruscan and Phoenician a dedication by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thefarie-velianas">Thefarie Velianas</a>, ruler of Caere. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dionysius-i-of-syracuse">Dionysius I of Syracuse</a> sacked the sanctuary in 384 BCE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pyrgi.jpg" alt="Pyrgi" width="320" height="237" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rjdeadly · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pyrgi_sanctuary.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Trade ran through coastal emporia under the protection of sanctuaries, such as Gravisca, the port of Tarquinia, and <b>Pyrgi</b>, a port of Caere.</blockquote><a href="/empire/etruscan-league">Etruscan League · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>At <b>Pyrgi</b>, the port of Caere, gold tablets of about 500 BCE record in Etruscan and Phoenician a dedication by Thefarie Velianas, ruler of Caere, to Uni, called Astarte in the Phoenician text.</blockquote><a href="/culture/etruscans">Etruscans · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li><li><blockquote>…parchment have perished, among them the annals of Tyre that Josephus quoted, leaving inscriptions on stone and metal, most of them short dedications and epitaphs; the royal texts of Byblos and Sidon and the bilingual inscriptions of Karatepe in Anatolia and <b>Pyrgi</b> in Italy are the chief exceptions.</blockquote><a href="/culture/phoenicians">Phoenicians · Arts &amp; Knowledge</a></li><li><blockquote>Caere looked to the sea through its ports, <b>Pyrgi</b>, Alsium and Punicum.</blockquote><a href="/state/caere">Caere</a></li></ul></section>
